      "stderr": {
        "path": "/Users/yong/.codex/worktrees/stemrobin-181-six/lemmadeck/.intentfold/tickets/STEMROBIN-181/tmp/d1816-d175-n12-local-text-repair/receipts/d175-003-prose-flow-en.stderr.txt",
        "bytes": 0,
        "sha256": "e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855"
      },
      "actualToolBoundaries": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "before": "If pressure, volume, and temperature all change at once, it is hard to find a clear pattern in an experiment.\nA simpler approach is to study a fixed amount of gas, keeping one of $p$, $V$, and $t$ constant while comparing the other two.\nA quantitative relationship between two of these variables, while the third stays constant, is called a gas law.",
          "after": "The original book credits the British scientist Boyle with discovering the first gas law and gives the year as $1660$.\nThat date is retained as the book's historical account, not as an independently verified date.\nThe book describes Boyle's work as \"New Experiments Concerning the Spring of Air.\""
        },
        {
          "index": 10,
          "before": "These observations led Boyle to propose that volume and pressure are inversely proportional when the amount of gas and its temperature stay constant.\nThe book also reports that observations with different amounts of mercury supported the relationship.\nThis is historical description, not a set of mercury-handling instructions for children.",
          "after": "The book then explains that the French physicist Mariotte reached the same conclusion later, independently of Boyle's work.\nIt therefore calls the relationship the Boyle-Mariotte law."
        },
        {
          "index": 16,
          "before": "The book gives hydrogen and oxygen as examples, along with mixtures such as air, to show that the relationship is not restricted to a single kind of gas.\nA modern explanation needs a limit: the relationship holds for a fixed amount of ideal gas at constant temperature, while real gases follow it approximately under suitable conditions, such as low density and temperatures away from condensation.\nThe book's phrase \"any gas\" does not mean every real gas obeys the law exactly at every pressure and temperature; departures can matter especially at high pressure or near condensation.",
          "after": "The book uses the apparatus in Figure $18$ to explain a test of the pressure-volume relationship in the Boyle-Mariotte law①.\nA sealed, corrugated container is connected to a pressure gauge $M$ that indicates the pressure inside; turning the screw rod $B$ changes the container's volume.\nThe position reading on ruler $L$ is related to the volume change and, in the book's apparatus model, is used to determine the volume."
        },
        {
          "index": 19,
          "before": "① The original book warns that repeating Boyle's mercury experiment requires stronger precautions: mercury vapor is toxic, and mercury spills easily.\nThis lesson explains the historical principle; it does not ask children to repeat the experiment or handle mercury.",
          "after": "Figure $19$ uses isotherms to show the pressure-volume relationship for the same fixed amount of gas.\nEach isotherm has one constant temperature, with absolute pressure inversely proportional to volume; in the ideal-gas model, this curve is called a hyperbola.\nReal gases approximate this relationship only within the limits explained earlier."
        }
      ],
      "paragraphCountReportedByTool": 22,
      "sectionAnchorsChanged": 0,
      "parserCalls": 1
    }
